WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.073
Back to the restaurant with four waiters and a board of order slips.

2
00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:10.867
Slips cost almost nothing, so every table gets a fresh one.

3
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:15.883
Then one evening a tour bus pulls up, and 5,000 orders arrive within a minute.

4
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.747
The waiters stay calm.

5
00:00:17.747 --> 00:00:19.095
The grill doesn't.

6
00:00:19.095 --> 00:00:23.437
It has room for 20 pans, and 5,000 orders want it at once.

7
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:26.361
Our shop has the same evening.

8
00:00:26.361 --> 00:00:31.665
Each order checks the stock, which takes 60 milliseconds and needs no limit.

9
00:00:31.665 --> 00:00:34.945
Then it saves the order in the database, in 10.

10
00:00:34.945 --> 00:00:39.692
The database takes only 20 connections, open lines to it, at a time.

11
00:00:39.692 --> 00:00:47.788
With a virtual thread per order and no limit, the lesson's test found 3,069 orders inside the fake database at once.

12
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.211
So here's the question many of us would quietly ask.

13
00:00:51.211 --> 00:00:52.816
Virtual threads are cheap.

14
00:00:52.816 --> 00:00:57.385
Can't we just make a pool of 20 of them, the way we always limited things?

15
00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:02.736
Let's be fair to that habit.

16
00:01:02.736 --> 00:01:03.368
It works.

17
00:01:03.368 --> 00:01:09.064
With 20 threads, the database never sees more than 20 orders, and nothing breaks.

18
00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:15.531
But each order keeps its pool thread for all 70 milliseconds, stock check included.

19
00:01:15.531 --> 00:01:19.908
So 1,000 orders take 1,000 over 20, times 70 milliseconds.

20
00:01:19.908 --> 00:01:21.191
How long is that?

21
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:26.712
About three and a half seconds.

22
00:01:26.712 --> 00:01:30.785
The lesson's demo measured 3,628 milliseconds.

23
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.449
In the restaurant, that's keeping only 20 slips, so each new table waits for a used one.

24
00:01:36.449 --> 00:01:38.926
The grill never holds more than 20 pans.

25
00:01:38.926 --> 00:01:42.394
Neither does the salad bar, which has room for everyone.

26
00:01:42.394 --> 00:01:46.728
The salad never touched the grill, and it still waited in line for it.

27
00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:51.029
So a pool caps the whole order, not just the one call that needs it.

28
00:01:51.029 --> 00:01:52.655
Never pool virtual threads.

29
00:01:52.655 --> 00:01:55.124
Put the limit on the thing that's scarce.

30
00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:59.576
So the kitchen hangs a hook beside the grill, with 20 metal tokens.

31
00:01:59.576 --> 00:02:06.415
Before an order goes on the grill, it takes a token, and when it comes off, the token goes back on the hook.

32
00:02:06.415 --> 00:02:12.558
An order that finds the hook empty waits on the board, which costs a slip of paper and no waiter.

33
00:02:12.558 --> 00:02:14.837
The salad bar needs no token at all.

34
00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:18.780
In Java, the hook is a Semaphore, a counter of permits.

35
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.535
Ours holds 20: the tokens.

36
00:02:20.535 --> 00:02:23.910
acquire takes a permit, and waits if none is left.

37
00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:27.488
release gives one back, and wakes one waiting thread.

38
00:02:27.488 --> 00:02:33.631
And a virtual thread that waits in acquire is parked on the heap, like a slip on the board.

39
00:02:33.631 --> 00:02:38.424
5,000 waiting orders cost 5,000 small objects, and no platform threads.

40
00:02:39.333 --> 00:02:45.603
Same 1,000 orders, one virtual thread each, and a Semaphore of 20 around the save alone.

41
00:02:45.603 --> 00:02:48.240
Now all the stock checks run at once.

42
00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.593
How long this time?

43
00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:59.843
60 milliseconds for every stock check together, then 50 rounds of 10 at the grill: about 560.

44
00:02:59.843 --> 00:03:01.612
The demo measured 604.

45
00:03:01.612 --> 00:03:05.229
Same limit on the database, six times faster.

46
00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:09.594
Here's the pattern in Java and Kotlin, with three rules.

47
00:03:09.594 --> 00:03:13.599
One: one semaphore per resource, in a field every order shares.

48
00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:16.142
Two: acquire on the line before the try.

49
00:03:16.142 --> 00:03:21.292
Three: release in the finally, so a save that throws still gives its permit back.

50
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:25.728
Now the restaurant picture breaks, and the break matters.

51
00:03:25.728 --> 00:03:27.828
A real token is a thing you hold.

52
00:03:27.828 --> 00:03:29.419
A permit is only a count.

53
00:03:29.419 --> 00:03:36.420
The semaphore never checks who gives one back, so a release from a thread that never got one makes 21 permits.

54
00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.029
That's why acquire stays outside the try.

55
00:03:39.833 --> 00:03:42.070
Rule one fails just as quietly.

56
00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:47.047
Picture a handler that makes a new Semaphore of 20 for every request.

57
00:03:47.047 --> 00:03:50.293
Every table gets its own hook with 20 tokens.

58
00:03:50.293 --> 00:03:51.303
Very generous.

59
00:03:51.303 --> 00:03:54.838
Nobody ever waits, and all 5,000 reach the grill.

60
00:03:54.838 --> 00:03:58.156
So the point is: one hook, shared by everyone.

61
00:03:58.967 --> 00:04:00.143
How many permits?

62
00:04:00.143 --> 00:04:03.809
Ask the resource, here the database's 20 connections.

63
00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:10.657
Then check with Little's law: 20 permits on a 50 millisecond call allow at most 400 calls a second.

64
00:04:10.657 --> 00:04:15.015
If orders come faster, the line on the board grows without end.

65
00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:18.313
And a customer rarely wants to wait forever.

66
00:04:18.313 --> 00:04:26.433
tryAcquire with a 200 millisecond timeout gives up if no permit comes in time, so the shop can say: busy, try again.

67
00:04:26.433 --> 00:04:30.143
In the lesson's demo, 1,000 requests arrived at once.

68
00:04:30.143 --> 00:04:35.813
100 were served, and the other 900 were turned away in about a third of a second.

69
00:04:35.813 --> 00:04:37.773
That's called load shedding.

70
00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.256
So, can't we just pool 20 virtual threads?

71
00:04:41.256 --> 00:04:44.229
We can, but then the salad waits for the grill.

72
00:04:44.229 --> 00:04:46.948
Threads are cheap, and the database is not.

73
00:04:46.948 --> 00:04:52.451
Give every task its own virtual thread, and every scarce resource one shared semaphore.

74
00:04:52.667 --> 00:04:53.550
Quick check.

75
00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:57.819
20 permits, and each database call takes 100 milliseconds.

76
00:04:57.819 --> 00:05:00.175
At most how many calls a second?

77
00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:03.537
200.

78
00:05:03.537 --> 00:05:07.425
20 permits divided by a tenth of a second.
